const intro={"n":31,"title":"The Confrontation Is Defused and the Family Prepares to Separate","nums":[1143],"summary":"After the Moreno and Fierro's charged exchange, people in the gathering intervene to prevent a physical fight. Fierro and the young men ride away and spend the night near a stream, sharing memories and sleeping outdoors with their horses and tack close at hand. Although their reunion brings joy, poverty prevents them from remaining together. They decide to separate and seek shelter and a livelihood individually. Before they depart, Fierro begins addressing advice to his sons and Cruz's son. The actual advice follows in Canto XXXII.","people":"Martín Fierro; his two sons; Picardía, identified as Cruz's son; the onlookers who prevent violence; the Moreno mentioned by the preceding confrontation.","themes":"De-escalation instead of revenge; family reunion and separation; poverty; gaucho sleeping customs; the transition from testimony to parental advice.","words":"arroyo (stream); carona (saddle pad); lomillo (saddle); cojinillo (soft saddle covering); poncho (outer garment); recao (riding equipment).","questions":["How is the threatened fight prevented?","Where do Fierro and the young men spend the night?","Which details describe traveling and sleeping outdoors?","Why do the young men decide to go separate ways?","To whom is Fierro about to give his advice?"],"analysis":"The entire canto is a single long stanza in the structured source, so it must not be broken into invented units. It is a bridge: no new murder occurs, and the father does not deliver the full series of maxims until the next canto. The source explicitly includes the son of Cruz among the listeners.","related":"/en/students/vuelta-canto-30/","relatedTitle":"The preceding contrapunto and threatened conflict"};
